entry.summary:
The NOT IN clause in SQL is useful for filtering out unwanted data. But it comes with caveats—mainly around NULLs, subqueries, and scalability. Here’s a breakdown of how NOT IN behaves in real scenarios and how to use it safely. Real-World Uses of NOT IN
These examples illustrate how NOT IN works in typical queries: Excluding known values:
SELECT * FROM company.invoices WHERE issued_by NOT IN ('Jack', 'Josh', 'Matthew');
Using a subquery for exclusions:
SELECT username FROM demo_table WHERE user_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM demo_table2);
Nested joins and subquery filtering:
SELECT * FROM purchases.suppliers WHERE supplier NOT IN ( SELECT supplier_id FROM old_purchases.suppliers INNER JOIN orders ON customers.customer_id = orders.customer_id );
